schema: claim-evidence
object:
  title: "Taxonomy first, ontology later — the strong default for small-team commons"
  type: claim-evidence
  claim: >-
    "For a team your size, the strong default is: ship a SKOS taxonomy now,
    and only reach for an ontology when a specific, recurring task can't be
    done by hand." Ask one question to decide: does anything need to reason
    over your relationships, or do humans just need to find things?
  evidence: >-
    Cited to SGKG's framing (taxonomy-as-classification vs. ontology-as-
    relationships, and how they layer) and Hedden Information Management's
    breakdown of the SKOS/OWL standards landscape; illustrated with Wikipedia's
    category system and Schema.org as taxonomy-scale examples vs. Wikidata as
    an ontology-scale example.
  source_lineage: src/content/docs/ontology-vs-taxonomy.md ([SGKG], [HEDDEN], [SCHEMA], [WIKIDATA] references)
  interpretation: >-
    A practical decision heuristic for the Regen Toolkit's own Layer 1
    (Ontology & Semantic Kernel) posture, which the article explicitly quotes:
    "not to create a perfect ontology before use" but enough shared meaning
    to avoid silently incompatible categories.
  uncertainty: >-
    This is editorial guidance from a knowledge-site article, not a
    peer-reviewed study — reasonable as a heuristic, not a proven rule for
    every commons.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/ontology-vs-taxonomy.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
